        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The overall objective of this proposal was to examine the hypothesis that cells exposed to microgravity undergo physical changes that affect the ability to sense direction, or have an altered polarity. One result of altered polarity in yeast is a changed budding pattern in Saccharomyces cerevisiae (Brewer’s yeast) and filamentation in Candida albicans (an opportunistic pathogen) consistent with increased pathogenicity.  Although our initial proposal included studies on S. cerevisiae and C. albicans, the C. albicans work became a priority based on interesting research results and the potential implications of the results as an opportunistic yeast pathogen.  Ultimately, phenotypic markers associated with increased virulence in C. albicans, and the environmental cues that trigger them, were evaluated following exposure to low-shear modeled microgravity.  The studies performed in this proposal were designed to be readily replicated in spaceflight conditions, should such an opportunity arise.  Indeed, as this project was concluding we received an opportunity to perform experiments with Candida albicans on the International Space Station so the final resources were utilized accordingly.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Our initial aim is to characterize the role of low shear modeled microgravity in S. cerevisiae gene expression and morphology. Progress on this aim has focused on the potential role of altered microenvironments produced secondary to the low shear bioreactor environment that may ultimately influence cellular metabolism.  
<p>
For this study, the plasmid 2DPT-G1(U) in S. cerevisiae strain D603 was used (in collaboration with Ross Carlson, PhD). The promoter-reporter plasmid (2DPT-G1(U)) expresses GFP under the control of the yeast GAL promoter, which is induced by galactose and repressed by glucose. If microenvironments do exist in LSMMG, it would be expected that nutrient diffusion to cells would be compromised, resulting in differential utilization of carbon sources compared to control conditions. This would be visualized as differential expression of the promoter-reporter plasmid. For these studies, cells were grown for 3 days in control or LSMMG conditions with complete medium (glucose), changing the medium daily. Since S. cerevisiae preferentially utilizes glucose rather than galactose, promoter-reporter expression provides information about carbon metabolism. 
<p>
Our second aim is to characterize the role of low shear modeled microgravity in C. albicans gene expression and morphology associated with increased virulence. Initial studies evaluated whether signaling pathway(s) that perceive changes in gravitational/low shear forces subsequently regulate genes with a role in filamentation and biofilm formation. A mitogen activator protein kinase (MAPK) inhibitor consistently reduced temperature (37C)- and serum-induced filamentation. In addition, immunoblot analysis demonstrated serum-responsive phosphorylation of an extracellular signal-regulated kinase (ERK)-like protein (likely CEK1) which provides an additional tool to evaluate the activation of this signaling pathway during exposure to the low shear environment. Involvement of the ERK-like pathway will now be evaluated in the low shear bioreactor-induced C. albicans filamentation. Conversely, we have not been able to demonstrate a consistent effect of the protein kinase A inhibitor, at several concentrations, on C. albicans filamentation. We suspect this is due to a lack of cross-species reactivity and will, therefore, evaluate other aspects of this signaling pathway. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The overall objective of this proposal was to examine the hypothesis that cells exposed to microgravity undergo physical changes that affect the ability to sense direction, or have an altered polarity. One result of altered polarity in yeast is a changed budding pattern in Saccharomyces cerevisiae (Brewer’s yeast) and filamentation in Candida albicans (an opportunistic pathogen) consistent with increased pathogenicity. Although our initial proposal included studies on S. cerevisiae and C. albicans, the C. albicans work became a priority based on interesting research results and the potential implications of the results as an opportunistic yeast pathogen. Ultimately, phenotypic markers associated with increased virulence in C. albicans, and the environmental cues that trigger them, were evaluated following exposure to low-shear modeled microgravity. The studies performed in this proposal were designed to be readily replicated in spaceflight conditions. Indeed, our proposal for a flight opportunity was approved and C. albicans was flown as Micro-6 on SpX CRS-1. The conclusion of this grant included a transition to the flight studies, which will be reported under separate cover. 
<p>
No data submitted. Summarized and analyzed data may be available through publications.
